From a63bbee2d010886ee5bcd98792a9571954b8e059 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 07:14:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kanban): a stale wake never moves a done card out of done

Invariant: a done or archived card leaves that status only through an explicit reopen. That means reopened,
unblocked, review_reopened, promoted_manual, or a status edit to a live status, ordered after its latest terminal
event in origin order (`_event_order`: second, UUIDv7 ms, id). A wake (continuation_woken, condition_met, promoted,
wake_check_*) is never a reopen, whatever order it was minted or delivered in.
hermes_cli/kanban_terminal_guard.py holds the rule (`terminal_hold`). It is enforced wherever a status is produced
or applied:

- Producers re-read the card's events in their write txn and do nothing for a held card, even when the local row
  still reads `scheduled`. These are `wake_due_continuations` (the parked sweep and the implied wake),
  `wake_check._wake` (condition_met/broken/lost) and `restore_missing_checks`. Each records one
  `stale_wake_ignored` per producer and terminal event.
- Push (`kanban_sync_client.push`): a Board-acked task row whose live `status` is held loses that field. The row's
  other fields still go.
- Feed apply (`apply_remote_changes`): at the end of the page, in its transaction, a card the page moved out of
  done/archived goes back if it is held, unless the Board's row is `running`. A reopen event arriving in a later page
  gives the held status back.
- Divergence repair (`_stale_lifecycle`): a run-less live status over the replica's held done card is the stale
  copy, not a local edit, so it is never pushed.
Every ignored wake stays in history, with a `stale_wake_ignored` event naming the terminal event it lost to.
The Board fences the lease too: daveey/hermes-hub#105.

Five whys (Board 11-11/12: 7 done cards re-leased in 36 h, every one with a continuation_woken and no reopen):
1. Why was t_01a1d1092f3caa2f leased again at 21:33 PST, 5 h after it completed? The Board row read `ready`.
2. Why `ready`? The MacBook's push carried `status=ready` from its 13:51:58 clock wake (event 716387), drained at
   21:3x, after worker-1's 16:14:37 completion (694876).
3. Why did a 13:51 status beat a 16:14 one? `push` stamps each op's HLC at send time (`_next_hlc`), so the drained
   op was the newest write to `status` in the Board's per-field LWW. The completion's event reached the MacBook's
   pull, but the row's `status` field was held back as a pending local edit.
4. Why did the MacBook wake a card that was done? Its pull was hours behind (pending 1733, ~71k feed rows; owner
   t_01a1d2cc49634a78), so its row still read `scheduled`. Worker-1's six cards (t_01a1c7b27ca3b496 and five more,
   done 10-30..11-10) had stale `scheduled` rows too. Its continuation sweep and wake_check restore woke them on
   11-11 14:22, and the Board leased them at 14:23.
5. Why did nothing stop it? No layer asked whether the card had reached a terminal status since the wake was minted.
   The producers read only the row. The push sends whatever the row holds. The apply takes the newer LWW field. The
   lease checks only `status === "ready"`.

Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_stale_wake_terminal.py has 11 tests.
- RED on origin/main 4e3dfef6869 (5 failed): (a) a wake minted before a completion and applied after it leaves the
  card done; (a) a wake pushed after a remote completion does not reopen the Board card (FakeService pair);
  (b) a restored wake / due continuation on a stale copy of a completed card does nothing.
- New guards with no main equivalent: probe condition_met; the implied wake; the divergence repair.
- Regression guards (d): an explicit reopen still reopens (same page and next page); a legitimate wake of a
  scheduled card (completed then reopened) still lands ready; terminal_hold orders by origin, not arrival.
- Mutants: removing each of the 8 guards (apply, push, continuation sweep, implied wake, restore, wake_check wake,
  divergence) fails its own test.
- Live: a scratch HERMES_HOME pair against a local `wrangler dev` Board. A stale continuation_woken is fired on the
  lagging gateway after the worker's completion, then its push drains. The Board stays `done`, the lease is refused,
  and there are 0 `claimed` events. On origin/main core the same run leaves the Board row `ready`.

